 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1031 "The Intersection of Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1033 2827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051033 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7c048599-260c-5867-88ec-692aa4d19c71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157086 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157086 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the sets $X = \\{2, 5, 7, 8 \\}$, $Y = \\{1, 3, 5 \\}$, and $Z = \\{2, 4, 6, 8 \\}$, find $X \\cap Y$, $Y \\cap Z$, and $Z \\cap X$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157086 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37d8d7b3-4252-5b87-899a-655137acdb4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157086 #edb/ref 1074 "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $X$ and $Y$. Since $X$ and $Y$ have only the element $5$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX∩Y &= {2, 5, 7, 8}∩{1, 3, 5} \\\\\n&= {5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Y \\cap Z$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Y$ and $Z$. Since $Y$ and $Z$ have no elements in common, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nY∩Z &= {1, 3, 5}∩{2, 4, 6, 8} \\\\\n&= {} \\\\\n&= ∅\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection $Z \\cap X$ is the set of all elements belonging to both $Z$ and $X$. Since $Z$ and $X$ have only the elements $2$ and $8$ in common, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ∩X &= {2, 4, 6, 8}∩{2, 5, 7, 8} \\\\\n&= {2, 8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157087 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157087 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ and $B$ be the set of all multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ and the set of all multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$, respectively. What is $A \\cap B$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a30b2847-895f-5a76-9e1d-dad43f528482" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157087 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA = \\{4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32, 36 \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB = \\{6,12,18,24,30,36,\\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nNow, $A \\cap B$ is the *intersection* of $A$ and $B$, which means that we want to find the elements that are in *both* $A$ and $B$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA∩B &= {4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 36, …}∩{6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, …} \\\\\n&= {12, 24, 36, …}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157094 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157094 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/dc/dcddc9ee6fe49965ea4f261fcb43c6f7ccd14193910e864b950745ef513e67a6.png)\n\nGiven the sets $X = \\{1, 2, 3, 5, 8 \\}$ and $Y = \\{1, 2, 4, 8 \\}$ shown above, find $X \\cap Y$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157094 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b33222a9-91db-51fd-b275-a6b593e2f6df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157094 #edb/ref 1074 "The intersection $X \\cap Y$ consists of all the elements that are in both $X$ and $Y$. To represent $X \\cap Y$, we will shade in all the area that is contained in both $X$ and $Y$.\n\n![](images/37/37243a3d1954c0ae7939c09ad05b6aa12a8dff8a3ae8915bbe2079051466cbcb.png)\n\nThe elements $1$, $2$, and $8$ are in both $X$ and $Y$. Therefore, the intersection of $X$ and $Y$ is\n\n$$\nX \\cap Y = \\{1,2,8 \\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157282 #edb/ref 1038 #ed